  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1224 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nDangerously hot and humid conditions persist across the Mid-South\nas a robust 598+ dam ridge remains centered over the western\nPlains and southern Rockies. Afternoon highs are forecast to\nreach the upper 90s areawide, with heat indices exceeding\n110 degrees again tomorrow. Consequently, an Excessive Heat\nWarning remains in effect through 8 PM tomorrow.\n\nA typical summertime pattern encompasses the CONUS, characterized\nby a strong ridge over the Plains and Intermountain West and a\nweak trough over the Northeast. This configuration is expected to\nremain largely stagnant over the next five days. However, a\ntrough originating in central Canada is projected to dive across\nthe northern Plains into the Midwest after midweek, facilitating\nthe erosion of the eastern periphery of the ridge and allowing\nslightly cooler conditions to return to the Mid-South.\n\nA deepening trough along the East Coast will help usher a cold\nfront into northwest Tennessee early Wednesday, progressing\nsouthwest into central Mississippi and Arkansas by the evening.\nWhile isolated showers or thunderstorms are possible, limited\nmoisture profiles will likely preclude widespread rainfall.\nTemperatures from Wednesday through the weekend will trend cooler\nas the western ridge shifts slightly further west. Highs through\nSaturday are forecast in the upper 80s to lower 90s, remaining\nbelow Heat Advisory or Warning criteria.\n\nModel guidance suggests a better potential for widespread\nrainfall by Saturday as a potent shortwave tracks across the\nDakotas into the Ohio Valley. An associated trailing cold front\nmay provide the most significant focus for regional rainfall in\nseveral days. However, given that summertime forecasting often\ndepends on mesoscale features below the resolution of numerical\nguidance, confidence in specific rainfall totals remains low.\nConfidence is higher regarding the continued cooling trend\nthrough the weekend, with high temperatures expected in the mid-\n80s to lower 90s Saturday and Sunday.",
